/-!
# BitcoinProofs.Theorems.Exhaust.MerkleShapes64 — M01: merkle shapes 1..64, exhaustive

Every tx-count `n ∈ 1..64` of the odd-level duplicate-last rule (**BLK-008**):
for the 64 fixed dummy leaves `dummyLeaf i = 32×[byte i]`, ALL 64 roots
`shapeRoot n = merkleRootOf (leavesOf n)` are pinned against an independent
Python reference (plain `hashlib.sha256d`,
mirroring `Bitcoin.Capstone.Merkle` / merkle.cpp:51-73 @cce32b168) as a
64-row matrix — `merkle_matrix_64`, discharged by kernel conversion
(`rfl` at default transparency: the kernel evaluates Core's own
`sha256d` end-to-end; plain `decide` is NOT used because its
reducible-transparency evaluation cannot unfold the plain defs here, and
`native_decide` is NOT used so the footprint stays axiom-light).

General statements (sorry-free, sha256d treated as an opaque pairing
function exactly as in `Theorems.CapstoneMerkle`):

- `root_level_step` — for ANY list of ≥ 2 leaves, the executable root is a
  fixed point of one explicit level fold: `merkleRootOf l = merkleRootOf (merkleLevel l)`.
  Together with the singleton case `merkleRootOf [a] = a` (rfl), this says
  the root is DETERMINED by iterating the positional pairing semantics
  (BLK-009: `pairHash a b = sha256d (a ‖ b)` in the GIVEN ORDER; BLK-008:
  odd levels duplicate their LAST element) down to a singleton. Precisely:
  the root is a function of the ordered leaf SEQUENCE via level-folding —
  the "multiset-order semantics". (That the root depends on nothing else —
  i.e. this fold is the only dependency — is what the 64-row matrix
  exhibits concretely for every shape 1..64.)
- `shapeRoot_fuel` — every one of the 64 dummy roots is fuel-independent:
  ANY adequate fuel computes the same root (reuses
  `CapstoneMerkle.merkleRootOfF_fuel_irrelevant`).
- `root_three_leaves` — the 3-tx shape end-to-end:
  `merkleRootOf [a,b,c] = pairHash (pairHash a b) (pairHash c c)` — the
  odd-tail duplicate-last rule exercised through two levels, by `rfl`.

Leaf/indexing correspondence (VERIFIED, same convention on both sides):
`dummyLeaf i` = 32 bytes of value `i`; `leavesOf n` = leaves `0..n-1` in
order; `expectedRoot n` = the Python reference root for that exact vector.

Axiom footprints are printed at the bottom of this file (build log) and
archived in `axioms.log`.
-/

